From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 07:50:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] count where the randomness comes from

HUMANLIKE.md has said since the look stack landed that "Everything with a random
source takes it through its constructor ... Every machine in a test run draws from
that run's seed, because a failure reported for a seed has to be reproducible by
running that seed." Nothing enforced it. The rule was prose, so a class could take
the clock in a field initialiser and the build would stay green while whatever it
paced became a thing you could see misbehave and never re-run - and every one of
those intervals is a humanlike one, so this is the count that protects the ability
to answer "do that again" about all the others.

RandomSourceTest scans the three source roots the way the write-counting tests scan
the hand's edges. A construction is either the clock read inside a constructor with
no argument - this(new Random());, fourteen of them - or it is on the test's list
with the reason beside it. Nine are on the list. Eight say plainly that there is no
seam yet: cadenceRand in InventoryBehavior and PathExecutor, the Cadence builders in
MineProcess, FarmProcess, GetToBlockProcess and BuilderProcess, and the Random fields
in BlockBreakHelper and BlockPlaceHelper. Each paces something a spectator can see
and cannot be driven from a seed, so a failure in that pacing cannot be re-run. That
is written into the entries themselves and into HUMANLIKE.md rather than dressed up:
the list is a record of a gap and should shrink as the gap closes.

The ninth is a reason, not a gap. AimScatter's SALT is a static utility's per-run
salt with no constructor to inject through, and its seam is the overload that takes
the salt for the tests, which AimScatterTest drives with a fixed 42L. A third test
fails if that overload goes, so the entry cannot quietly become a hole.

Two assertions were written first and removed. A per-class check that every
delegating class also declares a Random constructor cannot fire on code that
compiles - deleting TurnPace(Random) gives "constructor TurnPace in class TurnPace
cannot be applied to given types" - and it could false-positive on a signature
wrapped across lines, so javac keeps that job. What replaced it is a canary: if the
pattern is refactored away wholesale the count goes quiet rather than red, and a
count that reports nothing is indistinguishable from a count that found nothing
wrong.

Teeth: an unlisted `new Random()` field in LookBehavior fails exactly one test and
names the file and line, the other two staying green. 45 classes / 373 tests /
0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped, up from 44/370.

HUMANLIKE.md also gains the five conforming classes its list was missing - Flinch,
TurnPace, HotbarSwitch, InventorySwap, InventoryTransaction - which made it read as
though fourteen were nine.
